<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burghers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as burgher</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burghers were the citizens of chartered towns in medieval and early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, typically merchants and master craftsmen with civic rights. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> the dominant nobility restricted burgher political rights and economic protections. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> the burghers formed one of the four estates represented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>.</p></div>
